# Subtree attributes for the Ensemble COS body.
# Why: the deploy/launch/hook bash scripts must reach adopters with LF line
# endings even when cloned on Windows under core.autocrlf=true (a CRLF shebang
# breaks the script on Linux/macOS). Per-directory git attributes apply to this
# subtree and travel with it into the public projection (cos/** publishes
# wholesale, dotfiles included), so this fix ships with the tree and needs no
# publication-profile change; it also closes the latent hooks/*.sh exposure in
# the source repo.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The manifests (skills-shipped.txt, settings-shipped.txt) and the settings-merge
# fixture's JSON files each claim LF endings in their own header or in the
# fixture runner's. Without these lines a Windows clone under core.autocrlf=true
# would check them out with CRLF and make those claims false; the fixture
# compares its output byte for byte, so its inputs must be what they say.
*.txt text eol=lf
fixtures/**/*.json text eol=lf

# merge-settings.py is the one merge implementation both deploy scripts call,
# and its header pins it ASCII-only with LF endings like the scripts themselves.
# Without this line a Windows clone under core.autocrlf=true would check it out
# with CRLF and make that claim false. Python runs either way; the pin keeps the
# file what it says it is.
*.py text eol=lf
